# Scratch left at the repo root by an onboarding or debugging session.
#
# Not cosmetic: the Dockerfile does `COPY . .` and then `pnpm build`, so every
# file in the context is type-checked. A one-off probe importing
# `./lib/playwright` — a path that exists only in a scratch directory — failed a
# deploy of production code that was itself correct. Nothing reached the image
# and nothing broke; the cost was a build error about code that does not ship.
#
# Anchored to the root on purpose. These names are plausible inside src/ and
# worker/ (a real `check-*.ts` module, a real `*.out` fixture), and a pattern
# that swallowed one of those would fail the deploy just as hard and far less
# visibly. scripts/dockerignore.test.ts asserts no tracked file is caught.
/probe-*
/check-*
/debug-*
/scratch*
/tmp-*
/*.local.ts
/*.log
/*.bak
/*.orig
/*.rej
/*.tmp
/*.out
/*.har
/*.sql
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg
/*.webp
/*.gif
/*.pdf
/*.zip
/*.tar
/*.tar.gz
/*.dump.json
/*.bak.*
/*.ts.bak
# Config-writer one-offs from onboarding. `.gitignore` already has this exact
# pattern, so they are invisible to `git status` — and were sitting in the build
# context, unlinted and uncommitted, while this was written.
/write-*-config.js
